  "query_preview": "..\nIssued 233 PM CDT Thu Oct 22 2026\n\nSaturday through Thursday:\n\n500 mb cluster analysis continues to show an anomalous ridge\naxis over the Great Lakes region Saturday gradually shifting\neastward Sunday into Monday, with flow becoming more zonal\nacross the region. At the surface, south to southwest winds \nshould continue to maintain dry conditions and warm temperatures\nSaturday into Sunday. Highs should continue to be in the upper\n60s to lower 70s each day, with a southeast lake breeze possible\nSaturday afternoon near the lake. Ensemble members are\nsupportive of these values and the ensemble spreads are very \nsmall, indicating higher confidence.\n\nModels then show a cold front slowly shifting southeast toward\nor through the area Sunday night or Monday, with winds shifting\nto the northeast behind the front later Monday into Monday\nnight. The front may not have much moisture to work with, so any\nshower chances look minimal (under 15 percent) at this time.\nThere should be some clouds that occur during this time.\nTemperatures should cool off Monday with the northeast winds,\nwith highs more seasonable values in the upper 50s to middle \n60s.\n\nEnsemble members are rather mixed for shower chances through\nnext week, as there is disagreement with the timing and\nplacement of low pressure moving through the region. Kept NBM\nprecipitation chances for now (20 to 30 percent) for Wednesday\ninto Thursday, until more clarity can be found. Temperatures \nlook to be a little above seasonal normal values.\n\nWood",
